  "correctness_argument": "E. coli K-12 W3110 is the host of the Teteneva 2024 lake-water RB-TnSeq screen (#910), a K-12 derivative distinct from MG1655 and BW25113, and its assembly set ecoli_K12_W3110_ASM1024v1 was deposited in the genomes tier on 2026-10-09 (ten members, every md5 matched against NCBI's md5checksums.txt). A record written against W3110 must pin that set, so W3110 joins BacterialReferenceStrain and BACTERIAL_ASSEMBLY_SETS, the set id joins BacterialAssemblySet, and the accession pair joins ASSEMBLY_SET_ACCESSIONS as read off the deposited GCF assembly report (GCA_000010245.1 / GCF_000010245.2; the GCA report still names the 2006 RefSeq release GCF_000010245.1, whose files are not in the set). The authors mapped reads to the RefSeq replicon NC_007779.1 (quote below), so a W3110 record names the RefSeq accession. W3110's GenBank annotation (GCA_000010245.1) carries 4,444 gene features and not one locus_tag, so its only locus-tag namespace is the RefSeq one: ecoli_k12_w3110_refseq_locus_tag, pattern ^Y75_RS\\d{5}$, which every one of the 4,531 gene and pseudogene features of the deposited GCF flat file matches and which is disjoint from the four existing patterns and from yeast names (test_schema). A record keyed by a released symbol names this namespace for its host, as a heterologous symbol does. Each member is added, nothing is removed or renamed, and every existing record serializes as before; the change re-stales the bacterial dev stores that import these vocabularies (schema_impact: 58 classes, 0 breaking), none of them served in the newest release snapshot. A second W3110 release lands this vocabulary first: Potel 2018's phosphoproteome (#921) grew 'E. coli strain W3110' (the first author's dissertation, Chapter V, which reprints that paper), so its records pin the same set. This record and its symbols are carried into PR #1062 from the Teteneva branch commit b24d494b4 unchanged, without that commit's TransposonInsertionPerturbation.identifier_kind change, which awaits the owner's sign-off on #910 and is not part of this record.",
  "measurement_type_decision": false,
  "source_quotes": [
    {
      "value": "NC_007779.1",
      "provenance": {
        "source_uri": "paper/PMC11188689.1.txt",
        "citation_key": "tetenevaGenomewideScreenGenetic2024",
        "sha256": "422bd7926fbeebfd6877e15f213e18914291b1f259a7086d66d66d0249353b2f",
        "method": "the article's full text as the PMC Article Datasets bucket serves it (PMC11188689.1/PMC11188689.1.txt), mirrored verbatim in torchcell-raw",
        "page": "Materials and methods, 'Construction and characterization of the transposon mutant library'",
        "retrieved": null
      },
      "quote": "The reads containing the confirmed barcodes were then aligned to the E. coli K-12\u00a0W3110 reference genome (NCBI reference sequence: NC_007779.1)",
      "note": "the RefSeq replicon of GCF_000010245.2"
    }
  ],
  "alternatives_rejected": [
    {
      "alternative": "Pin W3110 records to the MG1655 assembly (b-numbers through the GenBank ECK:JW:b notes)",
      "why_rejected": "a W3110 record would claim another strain's genome; plan D9 refuses cross-strain inference, and the authors mapped to W3110's own NC_007779.1"
    },
    {
      "alternative": "Name the namespace after the GenBank annotation",
      "why_rejected": "the GenBank annotation carries no locus_tag at all (4,444 gene features, 0 tagged), so it defines no locus-tag namespace"
    }
  ],
